/** \file hciAnalyze_config.dox
 * \brief Configuration dictionary for hciAnalyze.
 */

/** \addtogroup hcianalyze_user_guide
 * @{
 *
 * \page hcianalyze_configuration_dictionary hciAnalyze configuration dictionary
 *
 * Long options use `--dotted.key value`; configuration files require a bare key under `[section]`, as described in
 * the shared configuration dictionary. The key/value columns use dotted shorthand only to keep the tables compact.
 *
 * \section hcianalyze_input Input and signal geometry
 *
 * | CLI | Config key/value shorthand | Type and limits | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|---|
 * | `-f`, `--file` | `file=/path/product.fits` | readable FITS image or cube | required | PSF-subtracted product to analyze. |
 * | `--lambdaD` | `lambdaD=2.5` | positive finite pixels per \f$\lambda/D\f$ | `2.5` with warning | Image sampling used to convert pixel radius into independent-resolution-element radius for the small-sample SNR correction. This is a top-level configuration key. |
 * | `--planet.sep` | `planet.sep=10,20` | nonnegative real-pixel vector | unset | Direct signal separations. Pair every value with `planet.PA`. |
 * | `--planet.PA` | `planet.PA=0,90` | finite degree vector | unset | Direct PAs east of north: north is up and east is left. |
 * | `--planet.contrast` | `planet.contrast=1e-4,1e-5` | optional matching nonnegative vector | unset | Physical planet contrasts included in output when supplied. Reduction-produced `PLANET*` headers always include contrast. |
 * | `--planet.R` | `planet.R=8` | one nonnegative real or one per signal | `10` | Signal exclusion radius or radii. |
 * | `--fake.sep` | `fake.sep=10,20` | nonnegative real-pixel vector | FITS keyword | Explicit fake separations; pair with `fake.PA`. |
 * | `--fake.PA` | `fake.PA=0,90` | finite degree vector | FITS keyword | Explicit fake PAs, east of north. |
 * | `--fake.contrast` | `fake.contrast=1e-4,1e-5` | one finite real per fake | FITS keyword | Fake contrasts included in output. |
 * | `--fake.R` | `fake.R=8` | one nonnegative real or one per fake | `10` | Fake exclusion radius or radii. |
 *
 * Do not mix explicit `planet` and `fake` settings. With neither group supplied, complete `PLANETSEP`, `PLANETPA`, and
 * `PLANETCONT` vectors take precedence. Otherwise the FITS header must provide all of `FAKESEP`, `FAKEPA`, and
 * `FAKECONT`. Header vectors are comma-separated and equal length. Direct planet configuration intentionally retains
 * compatibility with position-only analysis and does not require contrast metadata.
 *
 * \section hcianalyze_snr SNR and filtering
 *
 * | CLI | Config key/value shorthand | Type and limits | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|---|
 * | `--snr.minRad` | `snr.minRad=pixels` | finite real | `0` | Inner radial-noise bound. A non-positive value uses the minimum `REGMINR` or `HIERARCH P4 MIN RADIUS` header value. |
 * | `--snr.maxRad` | `snr.maxRad=pixels` | finite real | `0` | Outer radial-noise bound. A non-positive value uses the maximum `REGMAXR` or `HIERARCH P4 MAX RADIUS` header value. |
 * | `--snr.apertureR` | `snr.apertureR=pixels` | positive real | `2` | Radius of the aperture used to extract the reported `snr` value. |
 * | `--filter.hpfGaussFW` | `filter.hpfGaussFW=pixels` | finite real | `0` | High-pass Gaussian unsharp-mask FWHM; non-positive disables it. |
 * | `--filter.lpfGaussFW` | `filter.lpfGaussFW=pixels` | finite real | `0` | Low-pass Gaussian smoothing FWHM; non-positive disables it. |
 * | `--filter.psfResponse` | `filter.psfResponse=/path/psfResponse_manifest.fits` | complete P4 or KLIP PSF response manifest FITS path | unset | Apply the manifest's spatially varying signed response field to the input cube before SNR calculation. This permits a response field measured by one reduction to filter a compatible unsubtracted science product. |
 * | `-d`, `--diagnostics` | `diagnostics=true` | bool | `false` | Print resolved geometry, filters, invalid-pixel counts, and SNR values to standard error. |
 *
 * `filter.psfResponse` must name the `*_manifest.fits` file from a P4 or KLIP run with
 * `psfResponse.outputModels=true`, or an exact diagnostic manifest produced by the maintained
 * `build_klip_exact_response.py` tool. For P4, hciAnalyze locates the sibling `coordinates`, `model_NNNN`, and
 * `validity_NNNN` files. For KLIP schema 1, it locates the sibling `modeNNN_radial_response` and
 * `modeNNN_radial_validity` cubes. A sparse manifest reconstructs its global or region-aware radial interpolation. An
 * `EXACT_AZIMUTHAL` diagnostic manifest contains exactly one measured radius and a finite `KLIP PSF SAMPLE ANGLE`;
 * hciAnalyze preserves that detector-oriented response at the anchor angle and rotates it directly elsewhere. It
 * also accepts native schema-2 `PIXEL_EXACT` products containing a coordinate table and one response/validity plane
 * at every retained integer pixel. Those responses are applied only at their exact recorded coordinates. It rejects
 * incomplete sets and mismatched image planes, mode labels, stamp geometry, radial grids, regions, validity, or
 * product roles. With default identity noise, filtering uses the recorded `PSF FILTER MIN GOOD FRACTION` and the same signed normalized
 * estimator as the reduction: `sum(H*I)/sum(H*H)`. Pixels without a valid response remain excluded from the SNR
 * calculation.
 *
 * PSF-response filtering runs first, followed by high-pass filtering and then low-pass smoothing when those Gaussian
 * filters are also enabled. At image edges and beside invalid input samples, the Gaussian filters use partial valid
 * in-bounds kernels and renormalize by their retained weights.
 * The reported `snr` is the aperture maximum inside `snr.apertureR`; it is calculated as the signal pixel minus the
 * interpolated annular mean, divided by the interpolated annular standard deviation. `planet.R` or `fake.R` only
 * excludes the signal from the noise estimate. At pixel radius \f$r_{\rm pix}\f$, hciAnalyze uses
 * \f$n_2=2\pi r_{\rm pix}/{\tt lambdaD}-1\f$ comparison samples and multiplies the raw SNR by
 * \f$1/\sqrt{1+1/n_2}\f$. Pixels with \f$n_2\leq0\f$ are statistically undefined and set to zero. Omitting
 * `lambdaD` uses `2.5` pixels per \f$\lambda/D\f$ and prints a warning; explicitly setting `lambdaD=2.5` suppresses
 * that warning.
 * hciAnalyze always writes or replaces the full SNR cube beside the input as `<input-stem>_snr<input-extension>`.
 * Its header records the resolved signal geometry, the resolved SNR annulus and aperture, both filter FWHMs, and the
 * PSF response manifest and minimum support when configured. `LAMBDAD` records the adopted sampling and `SNRSMALL=1`
 * records that the small-sample correction was applied.
 *
 * \section hcianalyze_noise Conditional covariance weighting
 *
 * | CLI | Config key/value shorthand | Type and limits | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|---|
 * | `--noise.model` | `noise.model=pca` | `identity`, `diagonal`, `pca` | `identity` | Noise weighting for the supplied PSF response; identity preserves the original filter. |
 * | `--noise.outputDiagnostics` | `noise.outputDiagnostics=true` | bool | `false` | Also write conditional products for identity weighting. Diagonal and PCA always write them. |
 * | `--noise.arcStep` | `noise.arcStep=5` | finite nonnegative pixels | `0` | Training-center arc step; zero uses `(max(stampRows,stampColumns)-1)/2`, bounded below by one pixel. |
 * | `--noise.guardRadius` | `noise.guardRadius=2.5` | finite nonnegative pixels | `0` | Extra candidate exclusion beyond the enclosing circle of its response stamp. |
 * | `--noise.minimumSamples` | `noise.minimumSamples=8` | integer, 2 through `INT_MAX` | `8` | Minimum complete finite training patches. Overlapping patches are not independent samples. |
 * | `--noise.maximumModes` | `noise.maximumModes=3` | integer, 0 through `INT_MAX` | `3` | Maximum noise PCA modes above the variance floor; zero keeps only the floor with the trained mean. |
 * | `--noise.floorFraction` | `noise.floorFraction=0.1` | finite fraction in `(0,1]` | `0.1` | Absolute covariance floor is this fraction of the median training-pixel sample variance. |
 * | `--noise.excludeRows` | `noise.excludeRows=128,140` | finite vector | unset | Additional source/held-out centers along the first image index (display x). |
 * | `--noise.excludeColumns` | `noise.excludeColumns=150,160` | finite vector | unset | Additional source/held-out centers along the second image index (display y). |
 * | `--noise.excludeRadii` | `noise.excludeRadii=5,8` | matching nonnegative finite vector | unset | Radius of every additional exclusion circle, before training-footprint dilation. |
 *
 * Covariance weighting and requested conditional diagnostics require `filter.psfResponse` and disabled Gaussian
 * filters. All resolved `planet` or `fake` circles are excluded from training, together with the additional
 * circles above. Supply known sources explicitly when measured targets replace the header source list.
 *
 * Training uses the original final science image, before any matched filtering. Centers lie at the candidate's
 * radius, with equally spaced angles anchored on the positive first image axis. Their count is rounded upward
 * from circumference/requested spacing. Offsets rotate into the candidate's native response coordinates before
 * bilinear sampling, so the science and response stamps themselves are not resampled. Patches have the same radial
 * footprint across concentric reduction regions. The conservative training-footprint radius is the rectangular
 * stamp's half diagonal plus `sqrt(2)` pixels for the interpolation stencil. Every patch whose enclosing circle
 * intersects the candidate or another exclusion circle is discarded in full. Other incomplete/nonfinite patches
 * are discarded, with no zero filling. There is no taper or automatic overlap correction.
 *
 * Diagonal/PCA training subtracts the sample mean from science only and uses `n-1` variance normalization. PCA retains
 * the selected excess eigenvalues above a positive isotropic floor; its unmodeled complement keeps that floor.
 * Insufficient training, zero median variance, and invalid filter support remain invalid instead of falling back
 * to identity. The defaults are starting numerical settings, not empirically optimized detection settings.
 *
 * The following files are written beside the input with matching mode planes, as `<stem>_<role><extension>`:
 *
 * | Role | Quantity |
 * |---|---|
 * | `psf_amplitude` | Signed amplitude relative to the stored response normalization. |
 * | `psf_sigma` | Conditional amplitude uncertainty for a fixed supplied covariance and response. |
 * | `psf_score` | Signed correlation divided by the square root of the weighted normalization. |
 * | `psf_support` | Fraction of response pixels actually used. |
 * | `noise_samples`, `noise_attempted` | Accepted and proposed training-patch counts. |
 * | `noise_excluded`, `noise_incomplete` | Rejections from footprint exclusions and incomplete interpolation. |
 * | `noise_modes`, `noise_floor` | Actual retained rank and absolute variance floor. |
 * | `noise_status` | `0`: valid; `1`: too few samples; `2`: zero variance; `3`: invalid filter; NaN: no usable response. |
 *
 * Unavailable statistics remain NaN. Headers record the covariance settings, response manifest, resolved source
 * and held-out circles, sampling convention, and status definitions. Identity diagnostics use unit covariance,
 * zero mean, and zero training counts. `HCIA NOISE CALIBRATED=0` and `SNRSMALL=0` distinguish these conditional
 * products from the existing empirical annular SNR map, which still receives its small-sample multiplier.
 * Bilinear interpolation, overlapping training, and estimated covariance/mean all affect the null distribution;
 * `psf_score` is not a calibrated detection significance. Use held-out nulls and raw positive injections to measure
 * completeness at a fixed false-positive rate before selecting a preferred model.
 *
 * @}
 */
